-- title:
--   Interval exchange transformations, extensive amenability, recurrence and wobbling groups
-- statement:
--   The definitions behind the question whether the group of interval exchange transformations is amenable, after Juschenko, Matte Bon, Monod and de la Salle (JMMS, arXiv:1503.04977v1), Juschenko and de la Salle (JdlS, arXiv:1301.4736v4), and Lyons and Peres (*Probability on Trees and Networks*, 2016).
--
--   **Interval exchange transformations.** JMMS, p. 4: “An interval exchange transformation is a permutation of a circle obtained by cutting this circle into finitely many intervals (arcs), and reordering them. More precisely, an interval exchange transformation is a right-continuous permutation $g$ of $\mathbf R/\mathbf Z$ such that the set $\{gx - x, x \in \mathbf R/\mathbf Z\}$, called the set of angles of $g$, is finite.” On the circle $\mathbf R/\mathbf Z$ (`UnitAddCircle`), `IsIntervalExchange g` says that $g$ is right-continuous (`IsRightContinuous`: $t \mapsto g(x + t)$ is continuous from the right at $t = 0$, for every $x$), has finitely many angles $g(x) - x$ (`angles`), and has finitely many points of discontinuity. The last condition makes explicit the “finitely many intervals” of the first sentence, which the second does not imply (`IntervalExchange.exists_isRightContinuous_and_finite_angles_and_infinite_discontinuities`). JMMS, p. 4: “The set of all interval exchange transformations is a group acting on $\mathbf R/\mathbf Z$, denoted by IET.” `IET` is the subgroup of permutations of $\mathbf R/\mathbf Z$ generated by the interval exchange transformations; that it contains nothing else is a milestone.
--
--   **Angles and rank.** JMMS, p. 4: “Given a subgroup $G \le \mathrm{IET}$, we denote by $\Lambda(G) \le \mathbf R/\mathbf Z$ its group of angles, i.e. the subgroup of $\mathbf R/\mathbf Z$ generated by all increments $gx - x$ where $g \in G$ and $x \in \mathbf R/\mathbf Z$.” This is `angleGroup`. JMMS, p. 4: “Definition 1.7. The rational rank of $G$, denoted $\mathrm{rk}_{\mathbf Q}(G) \in \mathbf N \cup \{\infty\}$, is the supremum of all $d$ such that $\mathbf Z^d$ embeds in $\Lambda(G)$.” `rationalRank Λ` is that supremum for a subgroup $\Lambda$ of $\mathbf R/\mathbf Z$, so the rank of $G$ is `rationalRank (angleGroup G)`. JMMS, p. 17: “We denote $\mathrm{IET}(\Lambda)$ the subgroup of all $g \in \mathrm{IET}$ so that the angle $gx - x$ is in $\Lambda$ for every $x \in \mathbf R/\mathbf Z$.” This is `IETOf Λ`. `rotation a` is the rotation $x \mapsto x + a$, by which JMMS view $\Lambda(G)$ inside IET (p. 5).
--
--   **Amenable actions.** JMMS, p. 1: “Recall that an action of a group $G$ on a set $X$ is amenable if it admits a $G$-invariant mean, i.e. a finitely additive probability measure defined on all subsets of $X$ that is invariant under the action of $G$.” This is `IsAmenableAction`. JMMS, p. 1: “Definition 1.1. The action of a group $G$ on a set $X$ is extensively amenable if there is a $G$-invariant mean on $\mathscr P_{\mathrm f}(X)$ giving full weight to the collection of subsets that contain any given element of $\mathscr P_{\mathrm f}(X)$.” `IsExtensivelyAmenable G X` is the published `ThompsonAmenability.IsExtensivelyAmenableOn G X Set.univ`.
--
--   **Functors $I \to \mathrm{Amen}$.** JMMS, p. 2: “Denote by $\mathbf I$ the category of finite sets whose morphisms are injective maps. Denote further by $\mathbf{Amen}$ the category of amenable groups with group homomorphisms. Since $\mathbf{Amen}$ has direct limits, any functor $F\colon \mathbf I \to \mathbf{Amen}$ extends to the category of all sets with injective maps as morphisms by setting $F(X)$ to be the direct limit of $F(Y)$ as $Y$ runs over the directed set $\mathscr P_{\mathrm f}(X)$.” `FinInj` is the category of finite types and injective maps; a functor $F$ to groups is amenable-valued (`IsAmenableValued`) when every $F(A)$ is amenable; `extend F X` is the direct limit (a filtered colimit in groups) of the $F(A)$ over the finite subsets $A$ of $X$, and `extendMap` is the map induced by an injection. JMMS, p. 3: “Notice that any group action $G \curvearrowright X$ yields an action $G \curvearrowright F(X)$ by automorphisms and hence a semidirect product group $F(X) \rtimes G$. This semidirect product acts on $F(X)$ identified with the coset space $(F(X) \rtimes G)/G$.” These are `extendAut`, `FunctorProduct` and its action $(a, g)\cdot b = a \cdot g(b)$. JMMS, p. 3: “We say that the functor $F$ is tight on a nonempty set $X$ if for all (equivalently, some) $x \in X$ the morphism $F(X \setminus \{x\}) \to F(X)$ induced by inclusion is not onto.” This is `IsTight`, as in Definition 3.6 (p. 10), which allows every set. JMMS, p. 2: “Given $G \curvearrowright X$ one can form the permutational wreath product, or “lamplighter group”, $(\mathbf Z/2\mathbf Z)^{(X)} \rtimes G$. Here $(\mathbf Z/2\mathbf Z)^{(X)}$ denotes the group of finitely supported configurations $X \to \mathbf Z/2\mathbf Z$, on which $G$ acts by translations”; the lamplighter group (`Lamplighter`) acts on the configurations by $(f, g)\cdot f' = f + g f'$.
--
--   **Random walks.** JMMS, p. 14: “We can consider the (left) random walk $(g_n)_{n\ge0}$ on $G$ defined by $g_0 = e$ and $g_n = h_n g_{n-1}$ for $n \ge 1$, where $(h_i)_{i\ge1}$ are independent with law $\mu$. The inverted orbit $\mathbf O_n$ is then the (random) subset of $X$ $\mathbf O_n = \{x_0, g_1^{-1}x_0 \cdots, g_n^{-1}x_0\}$.” `walkPos` is $g_k = h_k \cdots h_1$, `invertedOrbit` is $O_n$, and `walkExp`, `walkProb` are expectations and probabilities, sums over the step sequences weighted by $\prod_i \mu(h_i)$. JMMS, p. 16: “a group action $G \curvearrowright X$ is called recurrent if for every symmetric, finitely supported probability measure $\mu$ on $G$ and every $x_0 \in X$, the random walk $(g_n x_0)$ on the orbit of $x_0$ induced by the left random walk on $G$ is recurrent.” `walkKernel` is the induced chain (from $x$ to $g x$ with probability $\mu(g)$), `IsRecurrentChain` says that its first-return probabilities sum to $1$, and `IsRecurrentAction` is the quoted notion. JMMS, p. 5: “We say that a probability measure on a group is non-degenerate if its support generates the group.” This is `IsNondegenerate`; `IsSymmetric` says $\mu(g^{-1}) = \mu(g)$, and `stepProb` is the $n$-step transition probability. JMMS, p. 5: “A group endowed with a probability measure $(G, \mu)$ is said to have the Liouville property if its Poisson–Furstenberg boundary is trivial.” `HasNontrivialBoundary μ` says that some bounded $\mu$-harmonic function on $G$ is not constant; the Liouville property itself is the published `ThompsonAmenability.IsLiouville`.
--
--   **Schreier graphs.** JMMS, p. 5: “Recall that the orbital Schreier graph of the action is the oriented labelled graph $\Gamma(G, X, S)$ whose vertex set is $X$ and edge set is $X \times S$, where the edge $(x, s)$ connects $x$ to $sx$.” `schreierBall S x₀ n` is the ball of radius $n$ about $x_0$ in this graph, the points $s_k \cdots s_1 x_0$ with $k \le n$ and $s_i \in S$, and `HasPolynomialGrowth` bounds its size by $C(n+1)^d$.
--
--   **Wobbling groups and metric spaces.** JdlS, p. 1: “Following [5] (see also [3]) we will call the group of all such bijections of $X$ the wobbling group of $X$ and denote it by $W(X)$”, the bijections $g$ with $|g|_w = \sup\{d(x, g(x)) : x \in X\} < \infty$ (JdlS (1)); this is `wobbling X`. JdlS, p. 1: “A metric space $X$ has bounded geometry if for every $R > 0$, the balls of radius $R$ have bounded cardinality.” This is `HasBoundedGeometry`, with closed balls. JdlS, p. 2: “Definition 1.3. Let $(X, d)$ be a metric space with bounded geometry and fix $x_0 \in X$. $(X, d)$ is called transient if there is $R > 0$ such that the random walk starting at $x_0$ and jumping from a point $x$ uniformly to $B(x, R)$ is transient. Otherwise it is called recurrent.” `ballKernel` is that walk on the closed ball, and `IsRecurrentSpace X x₀` says it is recurrent for every $R > 0$. `ContainsLipschitzBinaryTree` says that some injective Lipschitz map sends the infinite rooted binary tree, with its path metric `binaryTreeDist`, into $X$ (JdlS, p. 2), and `CoarselyEmbedsIn X Y` says there are $f : X \to Y$ and $\rho_- \le \rho_+$ with $\rho_-(t) \to \infty$ and $\rho_-(d(x, y)) \le d(f x, f y) \le \rho_+(d(x, y))$ (JdlS, p. 2, “embeds coarsely”).
--
--   **Networks.** Lyons and Peres, p. 26, require of a network that $\sum_{y \sim x} c(x, y) < \infty$ at every vertex (2.6), “so that the associated random walk is well defined”. `IsNetwork` asks for nonnegative symmetric conductances with positive finite sum at each vertex and a connected graph of positive conductances; `networkWalk` moves with probability proportional to the conductance, and `IsTransientNetwork` says that the walk started at some vertex fails to return with positive probability. Lyons and Peres, p. 44: “we say that a map $\phi$ from the vertices of $G$ to the vertices of $G'$ is a rough embedding if there are constants $\alpha, \beta < \infty$ and a map $\Phi$ defined on the edges of $G$ such that (i) for every edge $\langle x, y\rangle \in G$, $\Phi(\langle x, y\rangle)$ is a nonempty simple oriented path of edges in $G'$ from $\phi(x)$ to $\phi(y)$ with $\sum_{e' \in \Phi(\langle x, y\rangle)} r'(e') \le \alpha r(x, y)$ and $\Phi(\langle y, x\rangle)$ is the reverse of $\Phi(\langle x, y\rangle)$; (ii) for every edge $e' \in G'$, there are no more than $\beta$ edges in $G$ whose image under $\Phi$ contains $e'$.” `IsRoughEmbedding` states (i) for the edges between distinct vertices, with resistances $r = 1/c$ and paths through distinct vertices (`pathEdges`), and (ii) for oriented edges and ordered pairs.
-- source:
--   Juschenko, K., Matte Bon, N., Monod, N. and de la Salle, M., Extensive amenability and an application to interval exchanges, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 38 (2018) 195–219, https://doi.org/10.1017/etds.2016.32 (arXiv:1503.04977v1, whose page numbers are used), pp. 1–7, 14–17, §1–§5 (IET, angles, rational rank, extensive amenability, functors I → Amen, inverted orbits, recurrent actions); also Juschenko, K. and de la Salle, M., Invariant means for the wobbling group, Bull. Belg. Math. Soc. Simon Stevin 22 (2015) 281–290, https://doi.org/10.36045/bbms/1432840864 (arXiv:1301.4736v4, whose page numbers are used), pp. 1–2 (wobbling groups, bounded geometry, recurrent metric spaces) and Lyons, R. and Peres, Y., Probability on Trees and Networks, Cambridge University Press, 2016, https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316672815, §2.6, p. 44 (rough embeddings)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_Garrido_Amenability
import Definitions.Def_ThompsonAmenability

/-!
# Interval exchange transformations, extensive amenability and recurrence

K. Juschenko, N. Matte Bon, N. Monod and M. de la Salle, *Extensive amenability and an
application to interval exchanges*, arXiv:1503.04977v1 (JMMS); K. Juschenko and M. de la Salle,
*Invariant means for the wobbling group*, arXiv:1301.4736v4 (JdlS); C. Garban, *Inverted orbits
of exclusion processes, diffuse-extensive-amenability and (non-?)amenability of the interval
exchanges*, arXiv:1804.01981v4; R. Lyons and Y. Peres, *Probability on Trees and Networks*, §2.6.

Conventions:
- The circle is `UnitAddCircle = ℝ/ℤ`; permutations act by `Equiv.Perm.applyMulAction`.
- A measure on a group is a finitely supported `μ : G →₀ ℝ` where the source says "finitely
  supported", and a function `μ : G → ℝ` otherwise; expectations over random walks are `tsum`s
  over the sequences of steps.
- Random walks: `h : Fin n → G` lists the steps `h_1, …, h_n` as `h 0, …, h (n-1)`; the left
  random walk is `g_k = h_k ⋯ h_1`.
- Probabilities of events of a Markov chain with transition function `P` are `tsum`s.
-/

namespace IntervalExchange

open scoped ENNReal

/-! ## Interval exchange transformations (JMMS pp. 4, 17) -/

/-- JMMS p. 4: `g` is right-continuous at every point of `ℝ/ℤ`. -/
def IsRightContinuous (g : UnitAddCircle → UnitAddCircle) : Prop :=
  ∀ x : UnitAddCircle,
    ContinuousWithinAt (fun t : ℝ => g (x + (t : UnitAddCircle))) (Set.Ici 0) 0

/-- JMMS p. 4: the set of angles `{g x - x : x ∈ ℝ/ℤ}` of `g`. -/
def angles (g : UnitAddCircle → UnitAddCircle) : Set UnitAddCircle :=
  Set.range fun x => g x - x

/-- JMMS p. 4: an interval exchange transformation, a permutation of `ℝ/ℤ` obtained by cutting
the circle into finitely many intervals and reordering them: a right-continuous permutation with
finitely many angles and finitely many points of discontinuity. -/
def IsIntervalExchange (g : Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle) : Prop :=
  IsRightContinuous g ∧ (angles g).Finite ∧ {x | ¬ ContinuousAt g x}.Finite

/-- JMMS p. 4: the group `IET`, here the subgroup of permutations of `ℝ/ℤ` generated by the
interval exchange transformations. -/
def IET : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle) :=
  Subgroup.closure {g | IsIntervalExchange g}

/-- JMMS p. 4: the group of angles `Λ(G)` of `G`, the subgroup of `ℝ/ℤ` generated by the
increments `g x - x` for `g ∈ G` and `x ∈ ℝ/ℤ`. -/
def angleGroup (G : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle)) : AddSubgroup UnitAddCircle :=
  AddSubgroup.closure {a | ∃ g ∈ G, ∃ x, a = g x - x}

/-- JMMS Definition 1.7: the rational rank of a subgroup `Λ` of `ℝ/ℤ`, the supremum of all `d`
such that `ℤ^d` embeds in `Λ`. -/
noncomputable def rationalRank (Λ : AddSubgroup UnitAddCircle) : ℕ∞ :=
  ⨆ (d : ℕ) (_ : ∃ f : (Fin d → ℤ) →+ Λ, Function.Injective f), (d : ℕ∞)

/-- The permutations of `ℝ/ℤ` all of whose increments `g x - x` lie in `Λ`. -/
def anglesIn (Λ : AddSubgroup UnitAddCircle) : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle) where
  carrier := {g | ∀ x, g x - x ∈ Λ}
  one_mem' := by intro x; simp
  mul_mem' := by
    intro g h hg hh x
    have := Λ.add_mem (hg (h x)) (hh x)
    simpa [Equiv.Perm.mul_apply, sub_add_sub_cancel] using this
  inv_mem' := by
    intro g hg x
    have := Λ.neg_mem (hg (g⁻¹ x))
    simpa [neg_sub] using this

/-- JMMS p. 17: `IET(Λ)`, the subgroup of all `g ∈ IET` such that the angle `g x - x` is in `Λ`
for every `x ∈ ℝ/ℤ`. -/
def IETOf (Λ : AddSubgroup UnitAddCircle) : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle) :=
  IET ⊓ anglesIn Λ

/-- JMMS p. 5: the rotation `x ↦ x + a` of `ℝ/ℤ`. -/
def rotation (a : UnitAddCircle) : Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle :=
  Equiv.addRight a

/-! ## Amenable and extensively amenable actions (JMMS pp. 1, 6) -/

section Actions

variable (G X : Type*) [Group G] [MulAction G X]

/-- JMMS p. 1: the action of `G` on `X` is amenable when there is a `G`-invariant mean on `X`,
here a `G`-invariant finitely additive probability measure on all subsets of `X`. -/
def IsAmenableAction : Prop :=
  ∃ m : Set X → ℝ≥0∞, Garrido.IsFinitelyAdditiveMeasure m ∧ m Set.univ = 1 ∧
    Garrido.IsInvariant G m

/-- JMMS Definition 1.1: the action of `G` on `X` is extensively amenable. -/
def IsExtensivelyAmenable : Prop :=
  ThompsonAmenability.IsExtensivelyAmenableOn G X Set.univ

end Actions

/-! ## Functors from finite sets to amenable groups (JMMS pp. 2–3, §3) -/

section Functors

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

universe u

/-- The injective maps between finite sets. -/
def injectiveMaps : MorphismProperty FintypeCat.{u} := fun _ _ f => Function.Injective f

instance : injectiveMaps.{u}.IsMultiplicative where
  id_mem X := fun a b h => h
  comp_mem f g hf hg := by
    intro a b h
    exact hf (hg h)

/-- JMMS p. 2: the category `I` of finite sets whose morphisms are injective maps. -/
abbrev FinInj : Type (u + 1) := WideSubcategory injectiveMaps.{u}

/-- JMMS p. 2: a functor `I → Amen`, here a functor to groups whose values are amenable. -/
def IsAmenableValued (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) : Prop :=
  ∀ A, Garrido.IsAmenable (F.obj A)

/-- A finite subset of `X`, as an object of `I`. -/
abbrev finsetObj {X : Type u} (A : Finset X) : FinInj.{u} := ⟨FintypeCat.of A⟩

/-- The morphism of `I` induced by an injective map `f : X ↪ Y` from a finite subset `A` of `X`
to a finite subset of `Y` containing `f '' A`. -/
def finsetMap {X Y : Type u} (f : X ↪ Y) {A : Finset X} {B : Finset Y} (h : A.map f ⊆ B) :
    finsetObj A ⟶ finsetObj B :=
  ⟨FintypeCat.homMk fun a => ⟨f a, h (Finset.mem_map_of_mem f a.2)⟩, by
    intro a b hab
    have := congrArg Subtype.val hab
    exact Subtype.ext (f.injective this)⟩

lemma finsetMap_apply {X Y : Type u} (f : X ↪ Y) {A : Finset X} {B : Finset Y}
    (h : A.map f ⊆ B) (a : A) : ((finsetMap f h).hom a : Y) = f a := rfl

/-- The finite subsets of `X`, ordered by inclusion, as a diagram in `I`. -/
def finsetDiagram (X : Type u) : Finset X ⥤ FinInj.{u} where
  obj A := finsetObj A
  map {A B} i := finsetMap (Function.Embedding.refl X) (by simpa using leOfHom i)
  map_id A := by ext a; rfl
  map_comp f g := by ext a; rfl

/-- JMMS p. 2: the extension `F(X)` of a functor `F : I → Amen` to an arbitrary set `X`, the
direct limit of the groups `F(A)` over the finite subsets `A` of `X`. -/
noncomputable def extend (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) (X : Type u) : GrpCat.{u} :=
  GrpCat.FilteredColimits.colimit.{u, u} (finsetDiagram X ⋙ F)

/-- The structure maps `F(A) → F(X)`. -/
noncomputable def extendι (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) {X : Type u} (A : Finset X) :
    F.obj (finsetObj A) ⟶ extend F X :=
  (GrpCat.FilteredColimits.colimitCocone.{u, u} (finsetDiagram X ⋙ F)).ι.app A

/-- Two structure maps into `F(X)` agree on the image of `F(A)`, wherever `A` is sent. -/
lemma map_finsetMap_comp_extendι (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) {X Y : Type u} (f : X ↪ Y)
    {A : Finset X} {B B' : Finset Y} (h : A.map f ⊆ B) (h' : A.map f ⊆ B') :
    F.map (finsetMap f h) ≫ extendι F B = F.map (finsetMap f h') ≫ extendι F B' := by
  classical
  have hB : B ⊆ B ∪ B' := Finset.subset_union_left
  have hB' : B' ⊆ B ∪ B' := Finset.subset_union_right
  have w := (GrpCat.FilteredColimits.colimitCocone.{u, u} (finsetDiagram Y ⋙ F)).w (homOfLE hB)
  have w' := (GrpCat.FilteredColimits.colimitCocone.{u, u} (finsetDiagram Y ⋙ F)).w (homOfLE hB')
  change F.map _ ≫ extendι F _ = extendι F _ at w w'
  have e : finsetMap f h ≫ (finsetDiagram Y).map (homOfLE hB) =
      finsetMap f h' ≫ (finsetDiagram Y).map (homOfLE hB') := by ext a; rfl
  rw [← w, ← w', ← Category.assoc, ← Category.assoc]
  erw [← F.map_comp, ← F.map_comp, e]

/-- The morphism `F(X) → F(Y)` induced by an injective map `f : X ↪ Y`. -/
noncomputable def extendMap (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) {X Y : Type u} (f : X ↪ Y) :
    extend F X ⟶ extend F Y :=
  (GrpCat.FilteredColimits.colimitCoconeIsColimit.{u, u} (finsetDiagram X ⋙ F)).desc
    { pt := extend F Y
      ι :=
        { app := fun A => F.map (finsetMap f (subset_refl (A.map f))) ≫ extendι F (A.map f)
          naturality := by
            intro A B i
            have hAB : A.map f ⊆ B.map f := Finset.map_subset_map.2 (leOfHom i)
            have e : (finsetDiagram X).map i ≫ finsetMap f (subset_refl (B.map f)) =
                finsetMap f hAB := by ext a; rfl
            change F.map _ ≫ F.map _ ≫ extendι F _ = (F.map _ ≫ extendι F _) ≫ 𝟙 _
            rw [Category.comp_id, ← Category.assoc]
            erw [← F.map_comp, e]
            exact map_finsetMap_comp_extendι F f _ _ } }

lemma extendι_extendMap (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) {X Y : Type u} (f : X ↪ Y)
    (A : Finset X) :
    extendι F A ≫ extendMap F f =
      F.map (finsetMap f (subset_refl (A.map f))) ≫ extendι F (A.map f) :=
  (GrpCat.FilteredColimits.colimitCoconeIsColimit.{u, u} (finsetDiagram X ⋙ F)).fac _ A

lemma extend_hom_ext (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) {X : Type u} {Z : GrpCat.{u}}
    {φ ψ : extend F X ⟶ Z} (h : ∀ A : Finset X, extendι F A ≫ φ = extendι F A ≫ ψ) :
    φ = ψ :=
  (GrpCat.FilteredColimits.colimitCoconeIsColimit.{u, u} (finsetDiagram X ⋙ F)).hom_ext h

lemma extendMap_refl (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) (X : Type u) :
    extendMap F (Function.Embedding.refl X) = 𝟙 _ := by
  apply extend_hom_ext
  intro A
  have h : A.map (Function.Embedding.refl X) ⊆ A := by simp
  have e : finsetMap (Function.Embedding.refl X) h = 𝟙 _ := by ext a; rfl
  rw [extendι_extendMap, Category.comp_id, map_finsetMap_comp_extendι F _ _ h, e,
    F.map_id, Category.id_comp]

lemma extendMap_trans (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) {X Y Z : Type u} (f : X ↪ Y) (g : Y ↪ Z) :
    extendMap F (f.trans g) = extendMap F f ≫ extendMap F g := by
  apply extend_hom_ext
  intro A
  have h : A.map (f.trans g) ⊆ (A.map f).map g := by simp [Finset.map_map]
  have e : finsetMap f (subset_refl (A.map f)) ≫ finsetMap g (subset_refl ((A.map f).map g)) =
      finsetMap (f.trans g) h := by ext a; rfl
  rw [extendι_extendMap, ← Category.assoc, extendι_extendMap, Category.assoc,
    extendι_extendMap, ← Category.assoc, ← F.map_comp, e]
  exact map_finsetMap_comp_extendι F _ _ _

/-- The automorphisms of `F(X)` induced by an action of `G` on `X`. -/
noncomputable def extendAut (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) (G : Type*) [Group G] (X : Type u)
    [MulAction G X] : G →* MulAut (extend F X) where
  toFun g :=
    { toFun := (extendMap F (MulAction.toPerm g : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding).hom
      invFun := (extendMap F (MulAction.toPerm g⁻¹ : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding).hom
      left_inv := fun a => by
        have e : (MulAction.toPerm g : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding.trans (MulAction.toPerm g⁻¹ : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding =
            Function.Embedding.refl X := by ext x; simp [smul_smul]
        have := congrArg (fun φ => φ.hom a) (extendMap_trans F
          (MulAction.toPerm g : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding (MulAction.toPerm g⁻¹ : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding)
        simp only [e, extendMap_refl, GrpCat.hom_id, GrpCat.hom_comp] at this
        exact this.symm
      right_inv := fun a => by
        have e : (MulAction.toPerm g⁻¹ : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding.trans (MulAction.toPerm g : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding =
            Function.Embedding.refl X := by ext x; simp [smul_smul]
        have := congrArg (fun φ => φ.hom a) (extendMap_trans F
          (MulAction.toPerm g⁻¹ : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding (MulAction.toPerm g : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding)
        simp only [e, extendMap_refl, GrpCat.hom_id, GrpCat.hom_comp] at this
        exact this.symm
      map_mul' := map_mul _ }
  map_one' := by
    ext a
    have e : (MulAction.toPerm (1 : G) : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding = Function.Embedding.refl X := by
      ext x; simp
    simp [e, extendMap_refl]
  map_mul' g h := by
    ext a
    have e : (MulAction.toPerm (g * h) : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding =
        (MulAction.toPerm h : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding.trans (MulAction.toPerm g : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding := by
      ext x; simp [mul_smul]
    show (extendMap F (MulAction.toPerm (g * h) : Equiv.Perm X).toEmbedding).hom a = _
    rw [e, extendMap_trans]
    rfl

/-- JMMS p. 2: the semidirect product `F(X) ⋊ G` for an action of `G` on `X`. -/
abbrev FunctorProduct (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) (G : Type*) [Group G] (X : Type u)
    [MulAction G X] :=
  SemidirectProduct (extend F X) G (extendAut F G X)

/-- JMMS p. 2: the action of `F(X) ⋊ G` on `F(X)`, identified with the coset space
`(F(X) ⋊ G)/G`: `(a, g) · b = a · g(b)`. -/
noncomputable instance functorProductAction (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) (G : Type*) [Group G]
    (X : Type u) [MulAction G X] : MulAction (FunctorProduct F G X) (extend F X) where
  smul p b := p.left * extendAut F G X p.right b
  one_smul b := by
    show (1 : FunctorProduct F G X).left * _ = b
    simp
  mul_smul p q b := by
    show (p * q).left * extendAut F G X (p * q).right b =
      p.left * extendAut F G X p.right (q.left * extendAut F G X q.right b)
    simp [SemidirectProduct.mul_left, SemidirectProduct.mul_right, map_mul, mul_assoc]

/-- JMMS p. 3 and Definition 3.6: `F` is tight on `X` when for every `x ∈ X` the morphism
`F(X ∖ {x}) → F(X)` induced by inclusion is not onto. -/
def IsTight (F : FinInj.{u} ⥤ GrpCat.{u}) (X : Type u) : Prop :=
  ∀ x : X, ¬ Function.Surjective (extendMap F (Function.Embedding.subtype fun y => y ≠ x)).hom

end Functors

/-! ## Random walks and Markov chains (JMMS §4) -/

section Walks

variable {G X : Type*} [Group G] [MulAction G X]

/-- A measure `μ` on `G` is symmetric when `μ (g⁻¹) = μ g` for all `g`. -/
def IsSymmetric (μ : G →₀ ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ g, μ g⁻¹ = μ g

/-- JMMS p. 5: `μ` is non-degenerate when its support generates `G`. -/
def IsNondegenerate (μ : G →₀ ℝ) : Prop :=
  Subgroup.closure (μ.support : Set G) = ⊤

/-- The expectation `E F(h_1, …, h_n)` for independent steps `h_i` of law `μ`. -/
noncomputable def walkExp (μ : G → ℝ) (n : ℕ) (F : (Fin n → G) → ℝ) : ℝ :=
  ∑' h : Fin n → G, (∏ i, μ (h i)) * F h

/-- The probability that the steps `(h_1, …, h_n)` lie in the event `A`. -/
noncomputable def walkProb (μ : G → ℝ) (n : ℕ) (A : Set (Fin n → G)) : ℝ :=
  walkExp μ n (A.indicator fun _ => 1)

/-- JMMS p. 14: the left random walk `g_k = h_k ⋯ h_1`, `g_0 = 1`. -/
def walkPos {n : ℕ} (h : Fin n → G) (k : ℕ) : G :=
  (((List.finRange n).filter fun i => i.val < k).map h).reverse.prod

/-- JMMS p. 14, (1): the inverted orbit `O_n = {x₀, g₁⁻¹ x₀, …, g_n⁻¹ x₀}`. -/
noncomputable def invertedOrbit [DecidableEq X] (x₀ : X) {n : ℕ} (h : Fin n → G) : Finset X :=
  (Finset.range (n + 1)).image fun k => (walkPos h k)⁻¹ • x₀

/-- The transition function of the Markov chain on `X` induced by a measure `μ` on `G`: from `x`
the chain moves to `g • x` with probability `μ g`. -/
noncomputable def walkKernel (μ : G → ℝ) (x y : X) : ℝ :=
  open Classical in ∑' g, if g • x = y then μ g else 0

/-- For a Markov chain with transition function `P` started at `x₀`: the probability of being
at `y` at time `k ≥ 1` without having visited `x₀` at the times `1, …, k`. -/
noncomputable def avoidProb {V : Type*} (P : V → V → ℝ) (x₀ : V) : ℕ → V → ℝ
  | 0, _ => 0
  | 1, y => open Classical in if y = x₀ then 0 else P x₀ y
  | k + 2, y => open Classical in if y = x₀ then 0 else ∑' z, avoidProb P x₀ (k + 1) z * P z y

/-- The probability that the chain started at `x₀` first returns to `x₀` at time `n + 1`. -/
noncomputable def firstReturnProb {V : Type*} (P : V → V → ℝ) (x₀ : V) : ℕ → ℝ
  | 0 => P x₀ x₀
  | k + 1 => ∑' z, avoidProb P x₀ (k + 1) z * P z x₀

/-- The chain with transition function `P` started at `x₀` is recurrent: it returns to `x₀`
with probability `1`. -/
def IsRecurrentChain {V : Type*} (P : V → V → ℝ) (x₀ : V) : Prop :=
  HasSum (firstReturnProb P x₀) 1

/-- JMMS p. 16: the action of `G` on `X` is recurrent when for every symmetric, finitely
supported probability measure `μ` on `G` and every `x₀ ∈ X`, the random walk `(g_n x₀)` on the
orbit of `x₀` induced by the left random walk on `G` is recurrent. -/
def IsRecurrentAction (G X : Type*) [Group G] [MulAction G X] : Prop :=
  ∀ μ : G →₀ ℝ, ThompsonAmenability.IsProbability μ → IsSymmetric μ →
    ∀ x₀ : X, IsRecurrentChain (walkKernel (μ : G → ℝ)) x₀

/-- The `n`-step transition probabilities `P^n(x, y)` of a Markov chain. -/
noncomputable def stepProb {V : Type*} (P : V → V → ℝ) : ℕ → V → V → ℝ
  | 0, x, y => open Classical in if y = x then 1 else 0
  | n + 1, x, y => ∑' z, stepProb P n x z * P z y

/-- JMMS p. 5: the random walk `(G, μ)` has non-trivial Poisson–Furstenberg boundary: some
bounded `μ`-harmonic function on `G` is not constant. -/
def HasNontrivialBoundary (μ : G →₀ ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ f : G → ℝ, (∃ C, ∀ g, |f g| ≤ C) ∧ ThompsonAmenability.IsHarmonicOn μ Set.univ f ∧
    ∃ g h, f g ≠ f h

end Walks

/-! ## Schreier graphs (JMMS p. 5) -/

section Schreier

variable {G X : Type*} [Group G] [MulAction G X]

/-- The ball of radius `n` about `x₀` in the orbital Schreier graph `Γ(G, X, S)`: the points
`s_k ⋯ s_1 x₀` with `k ≤ n` and every `s_i ∈ S`. -/
def schreierBall (S : Set G) (x₀ : X) (n : ℕ) : Set X :=
  {y | ∃ l : List G, l.length ≤ n ∧ (∀ s ∈ l, s ∈ S) ∧ y = l.prod • x₀}

/-- JMMS p. 6: the Schreier graph `Γ(G, X, S)` grows polynomially: the balls about a point
`x₀` have at most `C (n + 1)^d` elements. -/
def HasPolynomialGrowth (S : Set G) (x₀ : X) : Prop :=
  ∃ C d : ℕ, ∀ n : ℕ, (schreierBall S x₀ n).ncard ≤ C * (n + 1) ^ d ∧
    (schreierBall S x₀ n).Finite

end Schreier

/-! ## Lamplighter actions and wobbling groups (JMMS p. 2, JdlS pp. 1–2, Garban p. 2) -/

section Lamplighter

variable (G X : Type*) [Group G] [MulAction G X]

/-- `G` acts on the finitely supported configurations `X → ℤ/2ℤ` by translating them. -/
noncomputable def lampAut : G →* MulAut (Multiplicative (X →₀ ZMod 2)) where
  toFun g :=
    { toFun := fun f => Multiplicative.ofAdd
        (Finsupp.mapDomain (fun x => g • x) (Multiplicative.toAdd f))
      invFun := fun f => Multiplicative.ofAdd
        (Finsupp.mapDomain (fun x => g⁻¹ • x) (Multiplicative.toAdd f))
      left_inv := fun f => by
        simp only [toAdd_ofAdd]
        rw [← Finsupp.mapDomain_comp]
        simp only [Function.comp_def, smul_smul, inv_mul_cancel, one_smul]
        rw [show (fun x : X => x) = id from rfl, Finsupp.mapDomain_id]; rfl
      right_inv := fun f => by
        simp only [toAdd_ofAdd]
        rw [← Finsupp.mapDomain_comp]
        simp only [Function.comp_def, smul_smul, mul_inv_cancel, one_smul]
        rw [show (fun x : X => x) = id from rfl, Finsupp.mapDomain_id]; rfl
      map_mul' := fun f f' => by
        simp [Finsupp.mapDomain_add] }
  map_one' := by
    ext f x
    simp only [one_smul, MulEquiv.coe_mk, Equiv.coe_fn_mk, toAdd_ofAdd, MulAut.one_apply]
    rw [show (fun x : X => x) = id from rfl, Finsupp.mapDomain_id]
  map_mul' g g' := by
    ext f x
    simp only [MulEquiv.coe_mk, Equiv.coe_fn_mk, MulAut.mul_apply, toAdd_ofAdd]
    rw [← Finsupp.mapDomain_comp]
    simp [Function.comp_def, mul_smul]

/-- JMMS p. 2: the permutational wreath product, or lamplighter group, `(ℤ/2ℤ)^(X) ⋊ G`. -/
abbrev Lamplighter :=
  SemidirectProduct (Multiplicative (X →₀ ZMod 2)) G (lampAut G X)

/-- JMMS p. 2: the affine action of the lamplighter group on the lamp group `(ℤ/2ℤ)^(X)`,
`(f, g) · f' = f + g f'`. -/
noncomputable instance lampAction :
    MulAction (Lamplighter G X) (Multiplicative (X →₀ ZMod 2)) where
  smul p f := p.left * lampAut G X p.right f
  one_smul f := by
    show (1 : Lamplighter G X).left * _ = f
    simp
  mul_smul p q f := by
    show (p * q).left * lampAut G X (p * q).right f =
      p.left * lampAut G X p.right (q.left * lampAut G X q.right f)
    simp [SemidirectProduct.mul_left, SemidirectProduct.mul_right, map_mul, mul_assoc]

/-- JdlS p. 1 and Garban Definition 1.2: the wobbling group `W(X)` of a metric space, the
permutations `g` of `X` with `sup_x d(x, g x) < ∞`. -/
def wobbling (X : Type*) [PseudoMetricSpace X] : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm X) where
  carrier := {g | ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ x, dist (g x) x ≤ C}
  one_mem' := ⟨0, fun x => by simp⟩
  mul_mem' := by
    rintro g h ⟨C, hC⟩ ⟨D, hD⟩
    refine ⟨C + D, fun x => ?_⟩
    calc dist ((g * h) x) x ≤ dist (g (h x)) (h x) + dist (h x) x := dist_triangle _ _ _
      _ ≤ C + D := add_le_add (hC _) (hD _)
  inv_mem' := by
    rintro g ⟨C, hC⟩
    refine ⟨C, fun x => ?_⟩
    have := hC (g⁻¹ x)
    simpa [dist_comm] using this

end Lamplighter

/-! ## Metric spaces with bounded geometry (JdlS pp. 1–2) -/

section Metric

variable (X : Type*) [PseudoMetricSpace X]

/-- JdlS p. 1: `X` has bounded geometry: for every `R > 0` the balls of radius `R` have bounded
cardinality. -/
def HasBoundedGeometry : Prop :=
  ∀ R : ℝ, 0 < R → ∃ N : ℕ, ∀ x : X, (Metric.closedBall x R).Finite ∧
    (Metric.closedBall x R).ncard ≤ N

/-- JdlS Definition 1.3: the random walk on `X` jumping from a point `x` uniformly to
`B(x, R)`. -/
noncomputable def ballKernel (R : ℝ) (x y : X) : ℝ :=
  open Classical in
  if y ∈ Metric.closedBall x R then ((Metric.closedBall x R).ncard : ℝ)⁻¹ else 0

/-- JdlS Definition 1.3: `X` is transient if there is `R > 0` such that the random walk starting
at `x₀` and jumping from a point `x` uniformly to `B(x, R)` is transient, and recurrent
otherwise. -/
def IsRecurrentSpace (x₀ : X) : Prop :=
  ∀ R : ℝ, 0 < R → IsRecurrentChain (ballKernel X R) x₀

/-- JdlS p. 2: `X` embeds coarsely in the metric space `Y`: there is a map `f : X → Y` and
functions `ρ₋ ≤ ρ₊` with `ρ₋ t → ∞` as `t → ∞` such that
`ρ₋ (d(x, y)) ≤ d(f x, f y) ≤ ρ₊ (d(x, y))` for all `x, y`. -/
def CoarselyEmbedsIn (Y : Type*) [PseudoMetricSpace Y] : Prop :=
  ∃ (f : X → Y) (ρm ρp : ℝ → ℝ), Filter.Tendsto ρm Filter.atTop Filter.atTop ∧
    ∀ x y, ρm (dist x y) ≤ dist (f x) (f y) ∧ dist (f x) (f y) ≤ ρp (dist x y)

/-- The infinite (rooted) binary tree: its vertices are the finite binary words, and the distance
between two words is the length of the path between them through their longest common prefix. -/
def binaryTreeDist (u v : List Bool) : ℕ :=
  u.length + v.length - 2 * ((u.zip v).takeWhile fun p => p.1 = p.2).length

/-- JdlS p. 2: `X` contains a Lipschitz and injective image of the infinite binary tree. -/
def ContainsLipschitzBinaryTree : Prop :=
  ∃ (f : List Bool → X) (C : ℝ), Function.Injective f ∧
    ∀ u v, dist (f u) (f v) ≤ C * binaryTreeDist u v

end Metric

/-! ## Networks and rough embeddings (Lyons–Peres §2.6) -/

section Networks

variable {V V' : Type*}

/-- Lyons–Peres §2.1 and §2.6: a network on the vertex set `V`, given by its conductances
`c x y`: nonnegative, symmetric, with `0 < ∑_y c(x, y) < ∞` at every vertex (2.6), and
connected. -/
def IsNetwork (c : V → V → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ x y, 0 ≤ c x y) ∧ (∀ x y, c x y = c y x) ∧ (∀ x, ∃ y, 0 < c x y) ∧
    (∀ x, Summable (c x)) ∧ ∀ x y, Relation.ReflTransGen (fun a b => 0 < c a b) x y

/-- The random walk on a network: from `x` it moves to `y` with probability
`c(x, y) / ∑_z c(x, z)`. -/
noncomputable def networkWalk (c : V → V → ℝ) (x y : V) : ℝ :=
  c x y / ∑' z, c x z

/-- A connected network is transient when its random walk is transient: started at some
vertex, it fails to return there with positive probability. -/
def IsTransientNetwork (c : V → V → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ a, ¬ IsRecurrentChain (networkWalk c) a

/-- The oriented edges `(v_i, v_{i+1})` traversed by a path `v_0, …, v_k`. -/
def pathEdges (p : List V) : List (V × V) :=
  p.zip p.tail

/-- Lyons–Peres p. 44: `φ` is a rough embedding of the network `c` on `V` into the network `c'`
on `V'`, with constants `α, β` and edge paths `Φ`: (i) for every edge `⟨x, y⟩` of `c`, `Φ x y` is
a nonempty simple oriented path of edges of `c'` from `φ x` to `φ y` whose resistances sum to at
most `α r(x, y)`, and `Φ y x` is its reverse; (ii) every edge of `c'` lies in the image of at
most `β` edges of `c`. Resistances are `r = 1 / c`; edges are the pairs `x ≠ y` with
`c x y > 0`. -/
def IsRoughEmbedding (c : V → V → ℝ) (c' : V' → V' → ℝ) (φ : V → V') : Prop :=
  ∃ (α : ℝ) (β : ℕ) (Φ : V → V → List V'),
    (∀ x y, x ≠ y → 0 < c x y →
      2 ≤ (Φ x y).length ∧ (Φ x y).head? = some (φ x) ∧ (Φ x y).getLast? = some (φ y) ∧
        (Φ x y).Nodup ∧ (∀ e ∈ pathEdges (Φ x y), 0 < c' e.1 e.2) ∧
        ((pathEdges (Φ x y)).map fun e => 1 / c' e.1 e.2).sum ≤ α * (1 / c x y) ∧
        Φ y x = (Φ x y).reverse) ∧
    ∀ u v, 0 < c' u v →
      {p : V × V | p.1 ≠ p.2 ∧ 0 < c p.1 p.2 ∧ (u, v) ∈ pathEdges (Φ p.1 p.2)}.ncard ≤ β ∧
      {p : V × V | p.1 ≠ p.2 ∧ 0 < c p.1 p.2 ∧ (u, v) ∈ pathEdges (Φ p.1 p.2)}.Finite

end Networks

end IntervalExchange


